    {
      "title": "Trust Problems",
      "desc": "BGP believes its neighbours",
      "detail": "A router accepts what its neighbours announce. A network that announces addresses that aren’t its own, by mistake or on purpose, pulls their traffic towards it: in 2008 a Pakistani ISP took YouTube offline worldwide this way. With RPKI the holder of a prefix signs which AS may announce it, and routers that check drop any other. Filters on what each neighbour may announce do the rest.",
      "facts": [
        [
          "Risk",
          "Hijacks and leaks"
        ],
        [
          "RPKI",
          "Signed: who may announce"
        ],
        [
          "Also",
          "Filters per neighbour"
        ]
      ],
